WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.360
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4519.

2
00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:02.720
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:03.173
<v JAMIE DIGILORMO>Alright.

4
00:00:03.173 --> 00:00:04.987
<v CHARLOTTE WILLCOX>Okay we are recording.

5
00:00:04.987 --> 00:00:05.440
<v DIGILORMO>Recording?

6
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:05.893
Great.

7
00:00:05.893 --> 00:00:06.347
Alright.

8
00:00:06.347 --> 00:00:09.067
So today is September twenty-fourth, 2015.

9
00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:17.227
I'm Jamie Digilormo representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

10
00:00:17.227 --> 00:00:24.480
We're working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville [Louisiana].

11
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.920
Today we're at the Rigmaiden Rec Center on the Old Spanish Trail.

12
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.187
So I'm here today with . . .?

13
00:00:32.187 --> 00:00:34.000
<v MARY JANE JONES>Mary Jane Jones.

14
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.136
<v DIGILORMO>Mary Jane Jones.

15
00:00:35.136 --> 00:00:38.545
Thank you for agreeing to be interviewed, first off.

16
00:00:38.545 --> 00:00:42.333
And this is going to be, again, a short interview.

17
00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:51.803
And hopefully we'll be in touch to set something up at a later point to maybe get a little bit more of a lengthy interview,

18
00:00:51.803 --> 00:00:52.939
or follow up.

19
00:00:52.939 --> 00:00:53.318
<v JONES>Okay.

20
00:00:53.318 --> 00:00:55.212
<v DIGILORMO>If that's okay with you?

21
00:00:55.212 --> 00:00:55.591
<v JONES>Yes.

22
00:00:55.591 --> 00:00:59.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay so when and where were you born?

23
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.128
<v JONES>Opelousas, Louisiana.

24
00:01:00.128 --> 00:01:02.385
September the twenty-fifth, 1938.

25
00:01:02.385 --> 00:01:04.641
<v DIGILORMO>Well happy early birthday.

26
00:01:04.641 --> 00:01:05.769
<v JONES>Thank you.

27
00:01:05.769 --> 00:01:06.897
<v DIGILORMO>So Opelousas.

28
00:01:06.897 --> 00:01:07.462
Alright.

29
00:01:07.462 --> 00:01:11.974
So a little bit north of Lafayette [Louisiana].

30
00:01:11.974 --> 00:01:12.538
Correct?

31
00:01:12.538 --> 00:01:13.103
<v JONES>Yes.

32
00:01:13.103 --> 00:01:13.667
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

33
00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:18.179
And were you parents born there as well?

34
00:01:18.179 --> 00:01:21.000
Are they from there?

35
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.929
<v JONES>No. From the surrounding area but not Opelousas in particular.

36
00:01:24.929 --> 00:01:25.321
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

37
00:01:25.321 --> 00:01:27.679
And what were your parents' names?

38
00:01:27.679 --> 00:01:32.000
<v JONES>Octavia Martin, Sam Martin, and my father was Arthur Martin.

39
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:32.830
<v DIGILORMO>Arthur Martin.

40
00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:33.245
Okay.

41
00:01:33.245 --> 00:01:37.811
Let's see . . . And they were from you said the surrounding areas.

42
00:01:37.811 --> 00:01:39.887
And what about your grandparents?

43
00:01:39.887 --> 00:01:43.208
Were they from the Opelousas area as well?

44
00:01:43.208 --> 00:01:44.868
<v JONES>Around the area, yes.

45
00:01:44.868 --> 00:01:45.283
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

46
00:01:45.283 --> 00:01:49.849
So your family goes back a few generations in that area.

47
00:01:49.849 --> 00:01:50.264
<v JONES>Yes.

48
00:01:50.264 --> 00:01:50.679
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

49
00:01:50.679 --> 00:01:54.000
So when did you move to Mossville?

50
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.840
<v JONES>In August of 1960.

51
00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.060
Well, I didn't live here in Mossville.

52
00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.440
<v DIGILORMO>Oh, you didn't?

53
00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.740
<v JONES>I lived in Vinton [Louisiana].

54
00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:04.580
<v DIGILORMO>You lived in Vinton.

55
00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:05.040
Okay.

56
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:06.420
You taught here?

57
00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.100
<v JONES>And I just traveled from Vinton to Mossville.

58
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:10.560
Yes.

59
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.400
<v DIGILORMO>Okay, I got you.

60
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.000
So then what was your earliest memory of Mossville?

61
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.714
<v JONES>Arriving here for my first job after graduating from college.

62
00:02:20.714 --> 00:02:24.057
This was my first time coming into this area.

63
00:02:24.057 --> 00:02:25.171
<v DIGILORMO>What’d you think?

64
00:02:25.171 --> 00:02:35.943
<v JONES>The beginning it was a little scary because it was my first job and there were a couple of students that . . . A couple of years older than I was.

65
00:02:35.943 --> 00:02:40.029
So a couple of them my same age so it was . . .

66
00:02:40.029 --> 00:02:40.400
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

67
00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.000
<v JONES>. . . a little scary at the beginning.

68
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:43.966
<v DIGILORMO>I can imagine so.

69
00:02:43.966 --> 00:02:44.207
<v JONES>Yes.

70
00:02:44.207 --> 00:02:46.138
<v DIGILORMO>So you taught in Vinton prior to that?

71
00:02:46.138 --> 00:02:47.103
<v JONES>No, no. This was . . .

72
00:02:47.103 --> 00:02:48.310
<v DIGILORMO>You just moved to Vinton.

73
00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:49.517
<v JONES>. . . my first job in Mossville.

74
00:02:49.517 --> 00:02:50.000
Yes.

75
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:50.714
<v DIGILORMO>Got you.

76
00:02:50.714 --> 00:02:51.429
In Mossville.

77
00:02:51.429 --> 00:02:53.929
<v JONES>My husband was working at Vinton School.

78
00:02:53.929 --> 00:02:54.286
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

79
00:02:54.286 --> 00:02:56.071
<v JONES>That was his first job.

80
00:02:56.071 --> 00:02:58.214
That's how we moved to Vinton.

81
00:02:58.214 --> 00:02:59.286
<v DIGILORMO>I got you.

82
00:02:59.286 --> 00:03:00.000
<v JONES>Yes.

83
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.167
<v DIGILORMO>And how old were you when you got married?

84
00:03:04.167 --> 00:03:04.630
<v JONES>Twenty.

85
00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:05.093
<v DIGILORMO>Twenty.

86
00:03:05.093 --> 00:03:05.556
Okay.

87
00:03:05.556 --> 00:03:06.019
Yeah.

88
00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:12.500
So, let's see, skipping over some of these since you didn't grow up here.

89
00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:16.204
What other . . . So you started to work here.

90
00:03:16.204 --> 00:03:20.833
Was it a high school at that point in time?

91
00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:21.296
<v JONES>Yes.

92
00:03:21.296 --> 00:03:21.759
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

93
00:03:21.759 --> 00:03:25.000
And what grades did you teach?

94
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.321
<v JONES>High school math.

95
00:03:26.321 --> 00:03:28.082
<v DIGILORMO>High school math, okay.

96
00:03:28.082 --> 00:03:35.127
And what did you . . . What were some of the initial experiences you had as a teacher?

97
00:03:35.127 --> 00:03:38.209
What did you think of the kids?

98
00:03:38.209 --> 00:03:42.172
Or . . . And the parents and that sort of thing?

99
00:03:42.172 --> 00:03:44.373
The community I guess really.

100
00:03:44.373 --> 00:03:52.739
<v JONES>Well I was pleasantly surprised because the students were very much geared toward education, the students that I had.

101
00:03:52.739 --> 00:03:56.701
I had some advanced classes and some not advanced.

102
00:03:56.701 --> 00:03:57.142
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

103
00:03:57.142 --> 00:04:02.425
<v JONES>The parents were very cooperative with the things I planned to do.

104
00:04:02.425 --> 00:04:09.910
So coming in as a new teacher I had to establish some ground rules in the beginning.

105
00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:12.993
Not buddy-buddy friendly, but down to business.

106
00:04:12.993 --> 00:04:24.000
Strictly to the point of even going . . . In the afternoons sometimes instead of going home I'd follow a kid home and meet their parents.

107
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:24.319
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

108
00:04:24.319 --> 00:04:26.870
<v JONES>So I would get to know the parents.

109
00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:27.188
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

110
00:04:27.188 --> 00:04:31.652
<v JONES>And that way it gave me a better understanding of what was going on.

111
00:04:31.652 --> 00:04:37.072
And one of the first things that I learned was not to go to the teachers’ lounge.

112
00:04:37.072 --> 00:04:38.667
It was too much gossip.

113
00:04:38.667 --> 00:04:42.174
I did not want anybody to tell me about a student.

114
00:04:42.174 --> 00:04:42.493
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

115
00:04:42.493 --> 00:04:46.000
<v JONES>I wanted to find out about that student for myself.

116
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:46.712
<v DIGILORMO>That's smart.

117
00:04:46.712 --> 00:04:50.983
<v JONES>So that helped a lot to get established as a teacher here.

118
00:04:50.983 --> 00:04:55.254
<v DIGILORMO>And how did the parents respond to such a hands-on involved approach?

119
00:04:55.254 --> 00:04:56.322
<v JONES>Oh they . . . Yes.

120
00:04:56.322 --> 00:04:57.390
They . . . Very excited,

121
00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:03.797
because they knew that if I had their child . . . At any time their child was in good hands.

122
00:05:03.797 --> 00:05:04.508
You know?

123
00:05:04.508 --> 00:05:04.864
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

124
00:05:04.864 --> 00:05:05.220
Right.

125
00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:07.000
<v JONES>Wanted to make sure.

126
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:07.526
<v DIGILORMO>Excellent.

127
00:05:07.526 --> 00:05:13.842
So did you teach up until the school became a junior high?

128
00:05:13.842 --> 00:05:17.000
Did you teach through that transition?

129
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:18.053
<v JONES>No, no.

130
00:05:18.053 --> 00:05:18.579
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

131
00:05:18.579 --> 00:05:21.737
<v JONES>A year before I was transferred.

132
00:05:21.737 --> 00:05:25.421
<v DIGILORMO>So in sixty-eight would that have been?

133
00:05:25.421 --> 00:05:25.947
<v JONES>Yes.

134
00:05:25.947 --> 00:05:27.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

135
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:32.143
<v JONES>And then we moved to Lake Charles [Louisiana] in sixty-nine from Vinton.

136
00:05:32.143 --> 00:05:36.429
And then I was . . . Went to work in Lake Charles.

137
00:05:36.429 --> 00:05:37.714
<v DIGILORMO>I got you.

138
00:05:37.714 --> 00:05:43.714
So how many years total were you at . . . Were you here in Mossville teaching?

139
00:05:43.714 --> 00:05:46.286
<v JONES>Approximately from sixty-three to . . . About five.

140
00:05:46.286 --> 00:05:49.714
Five and a half years more or less.

141
00:05:49.714 --> 00:05:50.143
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

142
00:05:50.143 --> 00:05:50.571
Wow.

143
00:05:50.571 --> 00:06:03.000
Do you remember any of the . . . Any significant changes in the school or even the community at large during those five or six years that you taught here?

144
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:10.794
<v JONES>Yes, I found that during that time . . . That at one time the students were very relaxed about not doing assignments,

145
00:06:10.794 --> 00:06:14.691
not getting work . . . not being afraid to be held back.

146
00:06:14.691 --> 00:06:20.926
That's why some of them were at that age or just dropping out for a year.

147
00:06:20.926 --> 00:06:22.875
But things seem to change.

148
00:06:22.875 --> 00:06:30.279
I think that we had a better quality of teachers that took more of an interest in the children.

149
00:06:30.279 --> 00:06:35.735
And doing that there were . . . And then coordinating with the parents made a difference.

150
00:06:35.735 --> 00:06:38.074
So they seem to excel more.

151
00:06:38.074 --> 00:06:48.985
And get involved in . . . I would always involve my student in the literary rallies and then take them to state so that gave them a sense of pride.

152
00:06:48.985 --> 00:06:54.831
And whatever that we could do to further their achievement, we’d try to do that.

153
00:06:54.831 --> 00:06:55.221
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

154
00:06:55.221 --> 00:06:56.000
<v JONES>Yes.

155
00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:56.357
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

156
00:06:56.357 --> 00:06:59.214
Well did you ever consider moving to Mossville?

157
00:06:59.214 --> 00:07:01.714
Or not . . . No, because you husband . . . Job.

158
00:07:01.714 --> 00:07:07.429
<v JONES>No because we had a home in Vinton and it was more convenient to just . . . To

159
00:07:07.429 --> 00:07:08.857
<c.p>travel back and forth.

160
00:07:08.857 --> 00:07:09.214
<v DIGILORMO>Yes.

161
00:07:09.214 --> 00:07:13.143
<v JONES>But I'll always take some of the students home on weekends.

162
00:07:13.143 --> 00:07:14.929
They laugh about that now.

163
00:07:14.929 --> 00:07:24.571
But especially during prom time I was considered the . . . I guess the person that if they couldn't get a date they could come and let me know,

164
00:07:24.571 --> 00:07:31.000
"This is the person I want to get a date Ms. Jones can you anything about it?"

165
00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:31.222
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

166
00:07:31.222 --> 00:07:33.444
<v JONES>And I would do that kind of stuff for them.

167
00:07:33.444 --> 00:07:34.556
<v DIGILORMO>Make it happen for them.

168
00:07:34.556 --> 00:07:35.000
<v JONES>Yes.

169
00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:37.384
<v DIGILORMO>That's a special teacher right there.

170
00:07:37.384 --> 00:07:41.356
Sounds like you want above and beyond for your students.

171
00:07:41.356 --> 00:07:44.932
<v JONES>Yes but we had some students sometimes that rebelled.

172
00:07:44.932 --> 00:07:48.904
I was grateful that I never had to encounter that,

173
00:07:48.904 --> 00:07:56.055
but I have had to stop some of them . . . Their aggressiveness with other teachers or with other students.

174
00:07:56.055 --> 00:07:56.452
<v DIGILORMO>Yes.

175
00:07:56.452 --> 00:08:04.000
<v JONES>So I was glad that I had that rapport with them that I was able to do that.

176
00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:04.455
<v DIGILORMO>Yes.

177
00:08:04.455 --> 00:08:05.821
They respected you.

178
00:08:05.821 --> 00:08:06.276
<v JONES>Yes.

179
00:08:06.276 --> 00:08:13.559
<v DIGILORMO>So listen to you . . . Had a little more weight behind your words than maybe other teachers.

180
00:08:13.559 --> 00:08:15.379
I could see that.

181
00:08:15.379 --> 00:08:25.848
So when you were teaching here, what were some of the establishments that you remember in Mossville as far as like grocery stores,

182
00:08:25.848 --> 00:08:29.945
or corner stores, or restaurants, or clubs, or anything?

183
00:08:29.945 --> 00:08:39.048
<v JONES>Actually nothing that I recall because I would always take the kids to Vinton and well . . . Wasn't much in Vinton.

184
00:08:39.048 --> 00:08:47.697
But we’d take them to Lake Charles, or to Orange, Texas for the movies and go to the beach.

185
00:08:47.697 --> 00:08:49.517
That type of thing.

186
00:08:49.517 --> 00:08:55.434
There was really nothing here recreational that would draw anyone to the area.

187
00:08:55.434 --> 00:08:56.345
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah, right.

188
00:08:56.345 --> 00:09:05.903
So I've heard a lot about the football team at Mossville and how successful they were, or how impressive they were.

189
00:09:05.903 --> 00:09:08.634
Were you teaching during that era?

190
00:09:08.634 --> 00:09:10.000
<v JONES>Oh yes.

191
00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.800
<v DIGILORMO>Of Mossville High School.

192
00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:14.950
So football was pretty big here then?

193
00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:15.400
<v JONES>Absolutely.

194
00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:15.850
<v DIGILORMO>Yes.

195
00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:16.300
<v JONES>Absolutely.

196
00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:18.100
And the band also.

197
00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:19.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

198
00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:19.403
<v JONES>Yes.

199
00:09:19.403 --> 00:09:21.821
<v DIGILORMO>They had a nice size band?

200
00:09:21.821 --> 00:09:22.224
<v JONES>Yes.

201
00:09:22.224 --> 00:09:22.627
<v DIGILORMO>Excellent.

202
00:09:22.627 --> 00:09:25.851
Well let's see how much time we got . . .

203
00:09:25.851 --> 00:09:27.866
<v WILLCOX>It's been about ten minutes.

204
00:09:27.866 --> 00:09:28.672
<v DIGILORMO>Ten minutes.

205
00:09:28.672 --> 00:09:29.075
Perfect.

206
00:09:29.075 --> 00:09:31.896
Alright well this will conclude our interview.

207
00:09:31.896 --> 00:09:33.910
Our short little preliminary interview.

208
00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:37.537
So thank you so much for talking with me.

209
00:09:37.537 --> 00:09:38.746
<v JONES>You're quite welcome.

210
00:09:38.746 --> 00:09:44.791
<v DIGILORMO>And I hope to set up a future appointment so we can talk some more.

211
00:09:44.791 --> 00:09:46.000
<v JONES>Very good.

212
00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:46.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4519.

213
00:09:46.429 --> 00:09:46.857
End Session I.]
